11:36:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Actually, no—and this is a great one to clear up because the *actual* naming history tells a pretty interesting (and almost ironic) story. ## The real reason melatonin got its name Melatonin was discovered in **1958** by dermatologist **Aaron Lerner** and his team. Here's what happened: - They isolated it from cow pineal glands while researching a skin condition (vitiligo) - When they tested this substance on **frog skin**, they found it caused **melanophores** (pigment-containing cells in frogs/amphibians) to **contract**, which made frog skin appear **lighter** - They named it "melatonin" as a nod to this melanophore-lightening effect they observed — combining "melano-" (referencing the melanophores/pigment cells it affected) + "-tonin" (related to serotonin, since melatonin is chemically derived from serotonin) ## So the name reflects: - A **historical laboratory observation** in frogs - An effect on pigment cells, but a **lightening** effect, not a darkening one - Its chemical relationship to serotonin (hence "-tonin," not just "-in") ## What this means for your question The name isn't saying "this hormone controls human skin tone." It's more like a fossil record of *how* it was discovered — through its interaction with amphibian pigment cells in a lab setting. That's genuinely different from "it's called that because it determines skin color." It's a bit like how "penicillin" is named after the *Penicillium* mold it was extracted from — the name tells you about *discovery/origin*, not necessarily the full scope of what the substance does in every context. So: the name does connect to pigment cells historically, but not in the way of "melatonin = the thing that makes human skin darker." If anything, the original lab finding pointed toward a lightening effect in frogs, not darkening — the opposite of what your original understanding suggested.
